        {
          "schema_version": 2,
          "ts": "2026-10-14T05:22:42.116058Z",
          "trace_id": "0f529cb79d5e45569ab8d55a9df804ab",
          "session_id": "full-g-md-001",
          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My home blood pressure has been around 150/95 for two weeks. Is that something I need to act on now, and what do the guidelines say the target should be?",
          "answer": "**If you have chest pain, shortness of breath, a sudden severe headache, weakness or numbness on one side, confusion, trouble speaking, or vision changes, call emergency services now.** You have not reported any of these.\n\nI could not finish the check of whether your reading needs action now or routine follow-up, so I can't tell you from this material which applies. Contact your clinician soon and share your home readings.\n\nUS cardiology guidance (*Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets*) classes an office reading of 140/90 or higher as stage 2 hypertension. A diagnosis is not based on one reading. It uses an average of two or more readings taken on two or more separate occasions. Home readings can help confirm this and can show white-coat hypertension (high only at the clinic) or masked hypertension (normal at the clinic but high at home). The retrieved passages do not give the cutoffs for home readings, so I can't say whether your two-week average meets them. Your clinician should compare your home log with office readings.\n\nFor most treated adults, US guidance sets a target below 130/80 mm Hg. Older adults with frailty, limited life expectancy, or other conditions may have an individualized target.\n\nGuidance differs on when treatment should start. US guidance treats much of the stage 1 range (130–139 or 80–89) as needing attention. A different body, in *Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line*, starts treatment at 140/90 and advises lifestyle measures alone for much of the population that US guidance calls stage 1. The retrieved passages do not give that body's numeric target, so I can't state it. The sources also do not grade how strong the evidence is for these thresholds.\n\nIf treatment is started, guidance suggests reviews about monthly until the target is reached, with kidney function and potassium checked after starting certain blood-pressure medicines. Any medicine decision 